  <text>
    <body>
      <div type="clause" n="31">
        <head><ref type="lawCode" target="law:IICn">II Cn</ref> 31</head>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="31" corresp="#wit-g-a">And hæbbe ælc hlaford his hiredmen on his ægenu<hi rend="italic">m</hi> borge.</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="ang" source="#liebermann" n="31" corresp="#wit-a">And habbe alc hlaford his hirdmen on his aganan borge.</ab>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="la" source="#liebermann" n="31" corresp="#wit-q">Et habeat omnis dominus familiam suam in plegio suo.</ab>
        <ab type="editionText" xml:lang="ang" source="#robertson" n="31" corresp="#wit-b">Be hiredmonnum. ⁊ hæbbe ælc laford his hiredmen on his agenum borhge.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#robertson" corresp="#wit-b">Concerning the men belonging to a household. And every lord shall be personally responsible as surety for the men of his own household.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="de" source="#liebermann">(Von Hausleuten. B) Und jeder Herr halte seine Hausleute unter seiner eigenen Verbürgung.</ab>
        <ab type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude" ana="#fromGerman">(Of household men. B) And let every lord keep his household men under his own surety.</ab>
        <note type="editorial">In B, ‘borhge’ was written above in the 12th century. Liebermann compares I Atr 1.10.</note>
      </div>
    </body>
